    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-old man is evaluated for a 5-year history of progressive cognitive decline that is causing significant impairment in daily activities. The patient''''s spouse reports that the patient''''s memory and alertness fluctuate dramatically from day to day, with some days being ''bright and normal'' and others characterized by profound confusion and sleepiness. Over the past year, the patient has experienced multiple well-formed, non-threatening **visual hallucinations** of children playing in the backyard. Physical examination reveals:<br>• Motor: <b>Bilateral resting tremor, cogwheel rigidity, shuffling gait, and postural instability (falls backward on pull test)</b><br>• Cognition: <b>MMSE score of 16/30</b><br><br>Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and what medication class must be avoided or used with extreme caution in this patient?', '["Vascular dementia; avoid high-dose antiplatelet therapy due to a high risk of intracranial hemorrhage", "Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB); avoid first-generation and typical/atypical antipsychotics due to severe neuroleptic hypersensitivity", "Alzheimer''''s disease with secondary parkinsonism; avoid cholinesterase inhibitors due to risk of worsening resting tremor", "Frontotemporal dementia; avoid selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) due to risk of serotonin syndrome", "Parkinson''''s disease; avoid levodopa-carbidopa therapy due to risk of inducing severe visual hallucinations"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This clinical presentation is highly classic for **Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)**. The core diagnostic features of DLB include: (1) **progressive cognitive decline** that interferes with normal functioning, (2) **fluctuating cognition** with pronounced variations in attention and alertness, (3) recurrent, highly detailed **visual hallucinations** (classically of people or animals), and (4) spontaneous **parkinsonism** (bradykinesia, resting tremor, cogwheel rigidity). A critical clinical hallmark of DLB is **severe neuroleptic hypersensitivity**: up to 50% of patients with DLB who are treated with antipsychotics (neuroleptics, such as haloperidol or risperidone) experience a severe, life-threatening worsening of parkinsonian motor features, sedation, autonomic instability, or features resembling neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Antipsychotics should be strictly avoided or used with extreme caution (such as very low-dose quetiapine or pimavanserin) if hallucinations are highly distressing.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-old female is brought to the cognitive neurology clinic by her family due to a progressive, 5-year history of behavioral and personality changes. The family reports that the patient has become increasingly **socially disinhibited**, making inappropriate sexual comments to strangers, and showing a profound **loss of empathy** toward family members. Her diet has changed dramatically, with the patient developing an intense **sweet tooth and hyperorality** (constantly eating cookies and stuffing her mouth with food). Ophthalmic and basic motor exams are normal. Neuropsychological evaluation reveals:<br>• Memory: <b>Slightly impaired, but significantly better than expected for the degree of behavioral change</b><br>• Executive function: <b>Profoundly impaired</b><br>• Brain MRI: <b>Bilateral, asymmetric atrophy of the frontal and anterior temporal lobes</b><br><br>Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)", "Alzheimer''''s disease (late-onset amnestic variant)", "Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH)", "Behavioral variant Frontotemporal Dementia (bvFTD)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient is presenting with the classic features of **behavioral variant Frontotemporal Dementia (bvFTD)**, which typically presents at an earlier age (commonly between 50 and 65 years) compared to Alzheimer''s disease. The diagnostic hallmarks of bvFTD include early and progressive: (1) **behavioral disinhibition** (inappropriate comments, loss of social decorum), (2) **apathy or inertia**, (3) **loss of empathy or sympathy** (interpersonal coldness), (4) **perseverative, stereotyped, or compulsive behaviors**, and (5) **hyperorality and dietary changes** (classically a strong craving for sweets, overeating, or stuffing food). Memory and visuospatial functions are characteristically **spared or preserved in the early stages** of the disease, while executive function is severely impaired. Brain MRI characteristically demonstrates **prominent atrophy of the frontal and/or anterior temporal lobes** (often referred to as ''knife-edge'' gyri).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-old male with a history of long-standing uncontrolled hypertension, type 2 diabetes mellitus, and a prior ischemic stroke is evaluated for memory problems. The family reports that the patient''''s cognitive decline has occurred in a **stepwise fashion**, with sudden drops in cognitive functioning following episodes of transient weakness or mild slurred speech. Neuropsychological testing demonstrates prominent **executive dysfunction** (impaired planning, organizing, and abstract thinking) that is out of proportion to a relatively mild memory impairment. Physical examination reveals:<br>• Neurological: <b>Mild pronator drift on the right, hyperreflexia in the lower extremities, and a positive Babinski sign on the right</b><br>• Brain MRI: <b>Multiple lacunar infarcts in the basal ganglia and extensive periventricular white matter hyperintensities (leukoaraiosis)</b><br><br>Which of the following represents the most likely diagnosis, and what is the primary management strategy to slow the progression of this condition?', '["Frontotemporal dementia; initiation of low-dose atypical antipsychotics to control behavioral shifts", "Vascular Dementia (VaD); aggressive control of cardiovascular risk factors (hypertension, lipids, diabetes) and antiplatelet therapy", "Alzheimer''''s disease; high-dose cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil) combined with memantine therapy", "Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus; urgent neurosurgical referral for ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt placement", "Dementia with Lewy Bodies; initiation of levodopa-carbidopa to treat vascular parkinsonism"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient''s presentation is highly characteristic of **Vascular Dementia (VaD)**, which is the second most common cause of dementia in older adults. Key diagnostic clues include: (1) a history of cardiovascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes, stroke), (2) a **stepwise cognitive decline** (sudden deteriorations followed by periods of stability), (3) **executive dysfunction** (e.g., impaired planning, processing speed) out of proportion to memory loss, (4) **focal neurological deficits** (e.g., asymmetric reflex changes, pronator drift, Babinski sign), and (5) MRI evidence of cerebrovascular disease (**multiple lacunar infarcts** or extensive white matter hyperintensities). The primary management strategy to prevent or slow the progression of VaD is **secondary prevention of cardiovascular and stroke risk factors**: aggressive blood pressure control (target <130/80 mmHg), lipid-lowering with statins, optimal glycemic control, smoking cessation, and **antiplatelet therapy** (e.g., aspirin).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-old male is brought to the outpatient clinic due to a progressive, 3-month history of gait instability, urinary problems, and cognitive changes. The patient''''s family describes the gait as ''magnetic,'' as if the patient''''s feet are glued to the floor, leading to 4 falls. The patient has also developed severe urinary urgency and occasional incontinence. Cognitive assessment reveals mild psychomotor slowing and forgetfulness. Physical examination and imaging reveal:<br>• Gait: <b>Slow, wide-based, short-stepped gait with difficulty initiating walking and turning</b><br>• Brain MRI: <b>Ventriculomegaly (enlargement of the lateral and third ventricles) out of proportion to cerebral cortical sulcal thinning</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, what is the best diagnostic test to predict surgical success, and what is the definitive treatment?', '["Vascular dementia; diagnosed by multi-infarct lesions on MRI, and treated definitively with aspirin and aggressive blood pressure control", "Progressive supranuclear palsy; diagnosed by vertical gaze palsy on neurological exam, and treated definitively with high-dose levodopa", "Parkinson''''s disease dementia; diagnosed by clinical response to a levodopa trial, and treated definitively with deep brain stimulation (DBS)", "Alzheimer''''s disease; diagnosed by CSF amyloid-beta and tau biomarker ratios, and treated definitively with daily donepezil", "Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH); diagnosed by gait improvement following a high-volume lumbar puncture (CSF tap test), and treated definitively with a ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient displays the classic clinical triad of **Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH)**: **gait disturbance** (magnetic, wide-based, short-stepped gait with difficulty initiating movement), **urinary urgency/incontinence** (''wet''), and **cognitive impairment/dementia** (''wacky''). This triad is classically summarized as **''wet, wobbly, and wacky''**, with gait impairment being the earliest and most prominent symptom. Brain MRI characteristically shows ventricular enlargement out of proportion to cortical sulcal atrophy. The gold-standard diagnostic and prognostic test is a **high-volume lumbar puncture (CSF tap test)**, where 30-50 mL of CSF is removed under normal opening pressure. A documented objective improvement in gait speed or balance 1-2 hours after the tap is highly predictive of a positive response to the definitive treatment, which is the surgical insertion of a **ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt**.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-old male is admitted to the neurology service due to a remarkably rapid, 4-week history of cognitive decline. The family notes that the patient was completely normal 4 weeks ago, but is now unable to dress hisself, feed hisself, or hold a conversation. Over the past week, the patient has developed involuntary jerking movements of the limbs, particularly when startled by loud noises or sudden lights. Neurological examination reveals:<br>• Motor: <b>Diffuse hyperreflexia, bilateral extensor plantar responses (positive Babinski), cerebellar ataxia, and prominent startle myoclonus</b><br>• Diagnostic studies: <b>EEG shows periodic, synchronous sharp-wave complexes; CSF is positive for 14-3-3 protein and RT-QuIC</b><br><br>Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)", "Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)", "Rapidly progressive Alzheimer''''s disease", "Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE)", "Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient''s clinical presentation represents a classic case of **Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)**, a rapidly progressive, fatal neurodegenerative disease caused by the accumulation of misfolded prion proteins (PrPSc). The key clinical clues are: (1) **rapidly progressive dementia** (developing over weeks to months, unlike typical neurodegenerative dementias), (2) **prominent myoclonus** (specifically **startle myoclonus**, where sudden auditory or tactile stimuli trigger brief, involuntary muscle jerks), (3) **cerebellar signs/ataxia** or visual disturbances, and (4) specific diagnostic findings. Diagnostic confirmations include: **EEG showing periodic, synchronous sharp-wave complexes** (PSWC), **CSF positive for the 14-3-3 protein** (a marker of rapid neuronal destruction) or **positive Real-Time Quaking-Induced Conversion (RT-QuIC)** assay (highly sensitive/specific for prions), and brain MRI showing hyperintensities in the cerebral cortex (''cortical ribboning'') and basal ganglia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-old female with mild cognitive impairment is brought to the clinic by her daughter due to a progressive worsening of confusion and daily forgetfulness over the past 2 weeks. The daughter reports that the patient was recently prescribed **Hydroxyzine (Atarax)** for **anxiety/insomnia** by another physician. On mental status examination, the patient''s MMSE score has dropped from 24/30 (measured 3 months ago) to **21/30**. Physical examination is notable for dry oral mucosa, mild tachycardia, and dilated sluggish pupils. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in clinical management?', '["Order a non-contrast CT scan of the brain and refer the patient for an emergent neurosurgical evaluation", "Increase the dose of Hydroxyzine (Atarax) as the patient''s anxiety and insomnia may be the primary cause of cognitive decline", "Slowly taper and discontinue Hydroxyzine (Atarax), advise non-pharmacological alternatives, and re-evaluate cognitive status in 4-6 weeks", "Initiate a trial of low-dose atypical antipsychotics (quetiapine) to treat the patient''s drug-induced psychosis", "Initiate donepezil (5 mg daily) immediately to compensate for the drug''s mechanism of action, and continue Hydroxyzine (Atarax)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient is experiencing **medication-induced cognitive impairment/delirium** secondary to **Hydroxyzine (Atarax)**. In older adults, especially those with pre-existing mild cognitive impairment (MCI) or dementia, drugs with **highly anticholinergic or sedative properties** (Beers Criteria medications) are common culprits of cognitive decline. In this case, **Hydroxyzine (Atarax)** (commonly prescribed for anxiety/insomnia) has potent anticholinergic activity, which directly blocks central acetylcholine receptors, causing acute confusion, dry mouth, tachycardia, and dilated pupils, and lowering the MMSE score. The first and most critical step in management is to **discontinue the offending medication** and offer safe non-pharmacological or alternative pharmacological therapies. A cholinesterase inhibitor (like donepezil) should *never* be added to ''treat'' the side effects of an anticholinergic drug.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-old woman with a history of hypertension and osteoarthritis is brought to the emergency department by her family due to a sudden change in mental status. The family reports that starting 5 days ago, the patient became increasingly confused, started seeing non-existent people in the room, and became highly agitated. They note that she is ''lucid and normal'' in the morning but becomes highly disoriented and sleepy in the late afternoon. Physical examination shows:<br>• Attention: <b>Severely impaired; unable to spell ''WORLD'' backward or recite the months of the year backward</b><br>• Consciousness: <b>Fluctuating level of arousal, ranging from somnolence to hypervigilant agitation</b><br>• Cognitive baseline: <b>Completely independent in all activities of daily living (ADLs) prior to 5 days ago</b><br><br>Which of the following clinical features is the most reliable in differentiating delirium from dementia in this patient?', '["Acute onset, fluctuating course of symptoms, and severe impairment in attention", "Impairment in short-term memory and disorientation to time and place", "Presence of visual hallucinations and nighttime agitation", "Symmetric resting tremor and bradykinesia during motor examination", "Inability to perform complex executive tasks and instrumental ADLs"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient is presenting with **delirium**, which is a clinical syndrome characterized by a **disturbance of attention and awareness** that develops over a short period of time (acute onset, typically hours to days) and tends to **fluctuate in severity** during the course of a day (classically worsening at night, known as ''sundowning''). The key clinical features that differentiate delirium from dementia are the **acute onset, the fluctuating course, and the prominent impairment in attention** (e.g., inability to spell ''WORLD'' backward or maintain focus). In contrast, dementia (major neurocognitive disorder) has an **insidious onset and progressive course**, with attention and consciousness typically remaining **intact** until the very advanced stages of the disease.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-old male with moderate Alzheimer''''s dementia is hospitalized for a severe urinary tract infection and subsequently develops hyperactive delirium. He is disoriented, attempting to pull out his intravenous catheter, and trying to climb out of bed. Vital signs and laboratory investigations are stable, and intravenous antibiotics are ongoing. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line intervention to manage this patient''''s delirium?', '["Implement non-pharmacological reorientation, provide sensory aids (glasses/hearing aids), maintain sleep-wake cycles, and employ a dedicated family member or sitter at the bedside", "Administer a dose of intramuscular lorazepam (0.5 mg) immediately and repeat every 8 hours as needed for agitation", "Apply soft bilateral wrist restraints and initiate continuous telemetry monitoring to ensure patient safety", "Prescribe scheduled low-dose intravenous haloperidol to rapidly control agitation and prevent catheter displacement", "Transfer the patient to a secure intensive care unit and initiate a low-dose continuous propofol infusion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The cornerstone and **first-line intervention for the management of delirium** is **non-pharmacological multicomponent environmental modification**. This includes: (1) **frequent reorientation** by staff and family, (2) providing the patient''s **sensory aids** (glasses, hearing aids) to reduce sensory deprivation/misperceptions, (3) **optimizing the sleep-wake cycle** (keeping the room bright during the day, keeping it dark and quiet at night, avoiding unnecessary nocturnal vitals or blood draws), (4) encouraging **early mobilization**, and (5) having a **familiar person or sitter at the bedside** (avoiding physical restraints at all costs). **Physical restraints are contraindicated** as they strongly exacerbate agitation, increase the duration of delirium, and increase the risk of injuries and pressure ulcers. Pharmacotherapy is reserved *only* for patients with severe agitation who pose an immediate danger to themselves or others.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 95-old female is brought to the clinic by her caregiver due to a 6-day history of acute confusion, visual hallucinations of animals in the room, and an inability to recognize family members. Prior to this, the patient was fully oriented and managed her own medications and finances. On physical examination, the patient is highly distracted and has a low-grade temperature of **101.3°F** (38.5°C). Which of the following is the most appropriate initial diagnostic approach for this patient?', '["Perform a formal 30-point Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) and refer the patient for outpatient neuropsychological testing", "Obtain vital signs, perform a detailed physical and neurological examination, conduct a comprehensive medication review, and order basic laboratory tests (electrolytes, glucose, CBC, urinalysis, and culture)", "Order an emergent non-contrast CT scan of the head followed by an immediate diagnostic lumbar puncture", "Prescribe a trial of donepezil (5 mg daily) and schedule a follow-up cognitive assessment in 6 months", "Order a high-density 24-hour ambulatory electroencephalogram (EEG) to rule out subclinical status epilepticus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient presents with **delirium**, which is an acute medical emergency. The diagnostic workup of delirium must be systematic and focused on identifying the **underlying medical cause(s)**, as delirium in older adults is almost always multifactorial. The **initial diagnostic approach** must include: (1) checking **vital signs** (to evaluate for fever, hypoxia, hypo/hypertension), (2) a thorough **physical and neurological examination** (evaluating for signs of infection like pneumonia or cellulitis, urinary retention, or focal neurological deficits), (3) a complete **medication review** (discontinuing any highly anticholinergic or sedative Beers Criteria drugs), and (4) **basic screening laboratory tests**, including serum electrolytes, blood glucose, complete blood count (CBC), and a **urinalysis with culture** (to screen for a urinary tract infection, a classic trigger in this age group). Neuroimaging (head CT) is reserved for patients with focal neurological signs, a history of recent head trauma, or unexplained refractory delirium.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-old female is evaluated for a 2-year history of progressive cognitive decline that is causing significant impairment in daily activities. The patient''''s spouse reports that the patient''''s memory and alertness fluctuate dramatically from day to day, with some days being ''bright and normal'' and others characterized by profound confusion and sleepiness. Over the past year, the patient has experienced multiple well-formed, non-threatening **visual hallucinations** of children playing in the backyard. Physical examination reveals:<br>• Motor: <b>Bilateral resting tremor, cogwheel rigidity, shuffling gait, and postural instability (falls backward on pull test)</b><br>• Cognition: <b>MMSE score of 21/30</b><br><br>Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis, and what medication class must be avoided or used with extreme caution in this patient?', '["Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB); avoid first-generation and typical/atypical antipsychotics due to severe neuroleptic hypersensitivity", "Frontotemporal dementia; avoid selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) due to risk of serotonin syndrome", "Parkinson''''s disease; avoid levodopa-carbidopa therapy due to risk of inducing severe visual hallucinations", "Vascular dementia; avoid high-dose antiplatelet therapy due to a high risk of intracranial hemorrhage", "Alzheimer''''s disease with secondary parkinsonism; avoid cholinesterase inhibitors due to risk of worsening resting tremor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This clinical presentation is highly classic for **Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)**. The core diagnostic features of DLB include: (1) **progressive cognitive decline** that interferes with normal functioning, (2) **fluctuating cognition** with pronounced variations in attention and alertness, (3) recurrent, highly detailed **visual hallucinations** (classically of people or animals), and (4) spontaneous **parkinsonism** (bradykinesia, resting tremor, cogwheel rigidity). A critical clinical hallmark of DLB is **severe neuroleptic hypersensitivity**: up to 50% of patients with DLB who are treated with antipsychotics (neuroleptics, such as haloperidol or risperidone) experience a severe, life-threatening worsening of parkinsonian motor features, sedation, autonomic instability, or features resembling neuroleptic malignant syndrome. Antipsychotics should be strictly avoided or used with extreme caution (such as very low-dose quetiapine or pimavanserin) if hallucinations are highly distressing.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-old male is brought to the cognitive neurology clinic by his family due to a progressive, 6-year history of behavioral and personality changes. The family reports that the patient has become increasingly **socially disinhibited**, making inappropriate sexual comments to strangers, and showing a profound **loss of empathy** toward family members. His diet has changed dramatically, with the patient developing an intense **sweet tooth and hyperorality** (constantly eating cookies and stuffing his mouth with food). Ophthalmic and basic motor exams are normal. Neuropsychological evaluation reveals:<br>• Memory: <b>Slightly impaired, but significantly better than expected for the degree of behavioral change</b><br>• Executive function: <b>Profoundly impaired</b><br>• Brain MRI: <b>Bilateral, asymmetric atrophy of the frontal and anterior temporal lobes</b><br><br>Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Behavioral variant Frontotemporal Dementia (bvFTD)", "Alzheimer''''s disease (late-onset amnestic variant)", "Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH)", "Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB)", "Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient is presenting with the classic features of **behavioral variant Frontotemporal Dementia (bvFTD)**, which typically presents at an earlier age (commonly between 50 and 65 years) compared to Alzheimer''s disease. The diagnostic hallmarks of bvFTD include early and progressive: (1) **behavioral disinhibition** (inappropriate comments, loss of social decorum), (2) **apathy or inertia**, (3) **loss of empathy or sympathy** (interpersonal coldness), (4) **perseverative, stereotyped, or compulsive behaviors**, and (5) **hyperorality and dietary changes** (classically a strong craving for sweets, overeating, or stuffing food). Memory and visuospatial functions are characteristically **spared or preserved in the early stages** of the disease, while executive function is severely impaired. Brain MRI characteristically demonstrates **prominent atrophy of the frontal and/or anterior temporal lobes** (often referred to as ''knife-edge'' gyri).'),
