ysfunction** (e.g., impaired planning, processing speed) out of proportion to memory loss, (4) **focal neurological deficits** (e.g., asymmetric reflex changes, pronator drift, Babinski sign), and (5) MRI evidence of cerebrovascular disease (**multiple lacunar infarcts** or extensive white matter hyperintensities). The primary management strategy to prevent or slow the progression of VaD is **secondary prevention of cardiovascular and stroke risk factors**: aggressive blood pressure control (target <130/80 mmHg), lipid-lowering with statins, optimal glycemic control, smoking cessation, and **antiplatelet therapy** (e.g., aspirin).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-old female is brought to the outpatient clinic due to a progressive, 2-month history of gait instability, urinary problems, and cognitive changes. The patient''''s family describes the gait as ''magnetic,'' as if the patient''''s feet are glued to the floor, leading to 2 falls. The patient has also developed severe urinary urgency and occasional incontinence. Cognitive assessment reveals mild psychomotor slowing and forgetfulness. Physical examination and imaging reveal:<br>• Gait: <b>Slow, wide-based, short-stepped gait with difficulty initiating walking and turning</b><br>• Brain MRI: <b>Ventriculomegaly (enlargement of the lateral and third ventricles) out of proportion to cerebral cortical sulcal thinning</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, what is the best diagnostic test to predict surgical success, and what is the definitive treatment?', '["Progressive supranuclear palsy; diagnosed by vertical gaze palsy on neurological exam, and treated definitively with high-dose levodopa", "Vascular dementia; diagnosed by multi-infarct lesions on MRI, and treated definitively with aspirin and aggressive blood pressure control", "Parkinson''''s disease dementia; diagnosed by clinical response to a levodopa trial, and treated definitively with deep brain stimulation (DBS)", "Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH); diagnosed by gait improvement following a high-volume lumbar puncture (CSF tap test), and treated definitively with a ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt", "Alzheimer''''s disease; diagnosed by CSF amyloid-beta and tau biomarker ratios, and treated definitively with daily donepezil"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient displays the classic clinical triad of **Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH)**: **gait disturbance** (magnetic, wide-based, short-stepped gait with difficulty initiating movement), **urinary urgency/incontinence** (''wet''), and **cognitive impairment/dementia** (''wacky''). This triad is classically summarized as **''wet, wobbly, and